1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,570
Show a three year old a picture
of a cat,

2
00:00:03,170 --> 00:00:04,870
and they'll say cat in an
instant.

3
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:09,080
For decades, that simple act
defeated the most powerful

4
00:00:09,090 --> 00:00:10,610
computers in the world.

5
00:00:11,333 --> 00:00:14,823
Today, AI systems recognise
faces, read

6
00:00:14,833 --> 00:00:17,633
medical scans, and steer cars
through traffic.

7
00:00:18,583 --> 00:00:20,883
So how did machines finally
learn to see?

8
00:00:21,343 --> 00:00:23,293
The answer came from copying the
brain,

9
00:00:23,303 --> 00:00:25,223
and from a mountain of pictures.

10
00:00:26,983 --> 00:00:30,183
To a computer, a picture is just
a grid of numbers:

11
00:00:30,723 --> 00:00:33,293
one for the brightness of each
tiny dot,

12
00:00:33,303 --> 00:00:34,103
or pixel.

13
00:00:34,917 --> 00:00:36,657
A cat might be black or ginger,

14
00:00:37,257 --> 00:00:38,157
near or far,

15
00:00:38,797 --> 00:00:41,757
curled up or stretching, half
hidden behind a sofa.

16
00:00:42,477 --> 00:00:45,077
Every version produces
completely different numbers.

17
00:00:45,917 --> 00:00:47,857
Early researchers tried writing
rules:

18
00:00:48,597 --> 00:00:51,737
a cat has two pointy ears,
whiskers, fur.

19
00:00:52,097 --> 00:00:54,197
But there were always too many
exceptions.

20
00:00:55,097 --> 00:00:56,297
The rules kept breaking.

21
00:00:57,167 --> 00:00:59,997
In nineteen sixty six, an MIT
professor

22
00:01:00,007 --> 00:01:03,267
famously set a group of students
a summer project:

23
00:01:03,807 --> 00:01:05,607
get a computer to describe what
it sees.

24
00:01:06,287 --> 00:01:08,047
It took more than half a
century.

25
00:01:09,733 --> 00:01:12,983
Meanwhile, neuroscientists were
discovering how real

26
00:01:12,993 --> 00:01:14,673
eyes and brains do it.

27
00:01:15,333 --> 00:01:18,823
In the late nineteen fifties,
David Hubel and Torsten

28
00:01:18,833 --> 00:01:22,203
Wiesel recorded signals from
single nerve cells in the

29
00:01:22,213 --> 00:01:23,773
visual part of a cat's brain.

30
00:01:24,583 --> 00:01:28,023
They found cells that fired only
when the cat saw an edge,

31
00:01:28,443 --> 00:01:30,403
tilted at one particular angle.

32
00:01:30,863 --> 00:01:34,303
Other cells combined those edges
into more complex shapes.

33
00:01:35,167 --> 00:01:37,567
The breakthrough came partly by
accident.

34
00:01:38,227 --> 00:01:41,217
A cell burst into activity as
they slid a glass

35
00:01:41,227 --> 00:01:44,677
slide into their projector, and
its edge cast a

36
00:01:44,687 --> 00:01:46,287
faint line across the screen.

37
00:01:47,083 --> 00:01:49,853
Vision is a ladder: from edges,
to shapes,

38
00:01:49,863 --> 00:01:51,063
to whole objects.

39
00:01:51,803 --> 00:01:55,073
That work won them a Nobel
Prize, and it inspired the

40
00:01:55,083 --> 00:01:57,103
computer scientists who came
next.

41
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,090
In nineteen fifty eight, a
psychologist named

42
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:04,390
Frank Rosenblatt built the
Perceptron, a

43
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:07,750
machine with a camera and a
simple artificial neural

44
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:10,280
network that could learn to tell
shapes apart.

45
00:02:11,083 --> 00:02:13,643
Its eye was a grid of just four
hundred light sensors,

46
00:02:14,083 --> 00:02:17,523
twenty by twenty, wired at
random to its artificial
neurons.

47
00:02:18,417 --> 00:02:20,677
Newspapers predicted it would
soon walk,

48
00:02:21,077 --> 00:02:22,117
talk and see.

49
00:02:22,617 --> 00:02:23,417
It couldn't.

50
00:02:23,657 --> 00:02:26,887
Its single layer of artificial
neurons was far too

51
00:02:26,897 --> 00:02:27,697
simple,

52
00:02:27,857 --> 00:02:30,697
and interest in neural networks
collapsed for years.

53
00:02:31,583 --> 00:02:32,703
Funding dried up.

54
00:02:33,123 --> 00:02:36,083
Researchers later called these
lean years the AI winters.

55
00:02:37,083 --> 00:02:40,403
In nineteen eighty, the Japanese
researcher Kunihiko

56
00:02:40,413 --> 00:02:43,553
Fukushima built the
Neocognitron, a layered

57
00:02:43,563 --> 00:02:47,033
network directly inspired by
Hubel and Wiesel's

58
00:02:47,043 --> 00:02:47,843
discoveries.

59
00:02:49,483 --> 00:02:52,733
An artificial neural network is
made of layers of

60
00:02:52,743 --> 00:02:53,543
simple units.

61
00:02:54,143 --> 00:02:56,873
Each one adds up signals from
the layer before,

62
00:02:56,883 --> 00:03:00,363
and passes a signal on if the
total is big enough.

63
00:03:01,250 --> 00:03:03,290
At first, the connections are
random.

64
00:03:03,890 --> 00:03:07,390
To train it, you show it
thousands of labelled examples.

65
00:03:08,070 --> 00:03:11,040
Every time it gets one wrong,
you nudge all the connections

66
00:03:11,050 --> 00:03:11,850
slightly,

67
00:03:12,350 --> 00:03:13,710
so it would have done a bit
better.

68
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:16,740
The method for working out those
nudges,

69
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:20,397
called backpropagation, was
popularised in nineteen

70
00:03:20,407 --> 00:03:23,480
eighty six by Geoffrey Hinton
and his colleagues.

71
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:27,490
In the late nineteen eighties,
Yann LeCun,

72
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:28,860
then at Bell Labs,

73
00:03:29,660 --> 00:03:32,140
built a network for reading
handwritten digits.

74
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:35,100
Instead of looking at the whole
image at once,

75
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:38,850
it scanned small windows across
it, like the edge-detecting

76
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:40,000
cells in the brain.

77
00:03:40,833 --> 00:03:43,133
This was a convolutional neural
network.

78
00:03:43,573 --> 00:03:46,463
By the late nineteen nineties,
versions of it were reading

79
00:03:46,473 --> 00:03:49,153
millions of handwritten cheques
for American banks.

80
00:03:50,083 --> 00:03:53,353
But for photos of the real
world, neural networks

81
00:03:53,363 --> 00:03:54,583
still weren't good enough.

82
00:03:55,143 --> 00:03:57,523
They needed two things that
didn't exist yet:

83
00:03:58,183 --> 00:04:00,763
far more data, and far more
computing power.

84
00:04:02,317 --> 00:04:05,602
The data came from a young
professor named Fei-Fei Li.

85
00:04:06,083 --> 00:04:09,493
She believed computers needed to
see the world the way children

86
00:04:09,503 --> 00:04:10,303
do:

87
00:04:10,503 --> 00:04:13,103
by looking at an enormous number
of examples.

88
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,150
Starting in two thousand and
seven, her team gathered

89
00:04:17,160 --> 00:04:20,410
images from the internet, and
paid thousands of online

90
00:04:20,420 --> 00:04:21,190
workers,

91
00:04:21,200 --> 00:04:24,110
through Amazon's Mechanical Turk
website,

92
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:25,140
to label them.

93
00:04:25,750 --> 00:04:29,072
At its peak, nearly fifty
thousand people in a hundred and
sixty

94
00:04:29,082 --> 00:04:32,490
seven countries were helping to
label the pictures.

95
00:04:33,417 --> 00:04:35,837
Many experts thought it was a
waste of time.

96
00:04:36,377 --> 00:04:37,177
More data,

97
00:04:37,237 --> 00:04:38,037
they argued,

98
00:04:38,477 --> 00:04:39,997
wouldn't fix bad algorithms.

99
00:04:41,083 --> 00:04:44,213
The result, ImageNet, held more
than fourteen

100
00:04:44,223 --> 00:04:47,493
million pictures, sorted into
over twenty

101
00:04:47,503 --> 00:04:50,633
thousand categories, from
strawberries to sports

102
00:04:50,643 --> 00:04:52,863
cars to dozens of breeds of dog.

103
00:04:53,667 --> 00:04:56,877
In two thousand and ten, she
launched a yearly

104
00:04:56,887 --> 00:04:57,597
contest:

105
00:04:57,607 --> 00:05:01,057
whose program could recognise
objects in

106
00:05:01,067 --> 00:05:03,767
a thousand different categories
most accurately?

107
00:05:05,567 --> 00:05:08,937
In twenty twelve, a team from
the University of

108
00:05:08,947 --> 00:05:12,377
Toronto entered: Geoffrey Hinton
and two of

109
00:05:12,387 --> 00:05:15,617
his students, Alex Krizhevsky
and Ilya

110
00:05:15,627 --> 00:05:16,427
Sutskever.

111
00:05:16,917 --> 00:05:20,137
Their network, later called
AlexNet, was deep,

112
00:05:20,637 --> 00:05:23,387
with eight learning layers and
sixty million adjustable

113
00:05:23,397 --> 00:05:24,237
connections.

114
00:05:25,250 --> 00:05:28,570
To train it, Krizhevsky used two
graphics cards,

115
00:05:29,110 --> 00:05:31,990
the chips built for video games,
in his bedroom.

116
00:05:32,510 --> 00:05:33,790
Training took about a week.

117
00:05:34,350 --> 00:05:37,400
Their parallel number crunching
turned out to be perfect for

118
00:05:37,410 --> 00:05:38,210
neural networks.

119
00:05:39,083 --> 00:05:40,753
The results stunned the field.

120
00:05:40,763 --> 00:05:43,913
AlexNet's top five error rate
was about

121
00:05:43,923 --> 00:05:45,133
fifteen percent.

122
00:05:45,143 --> 00:05:48,293
The next best entry got twenty
six percent.

123
00:05:48,833 --> 00:05:52,203
That same year, a Google team
trained a huge network

124
00:05:52,213 --> 00:05:55,523
on ten million still frames from
YouTube videos,

125
00:05:55,533 --> 00:05:57,873
without telling it what anything
was.

126
00:05:58,493 --> 00:06:00,473
One of its artificial neurons
learned,

127
00:06:01,133 --> 00:06:03,673
all by itself, to respond to
cats.

128
00:06:04,667 --> 00:06:07,627
Soon after, Google bought
Hinton's tiny company,

129
00:06:08,107 --> 00:06:11,097
which had just three employees,
for forty four million

130
00:06:11,107 --> 00:06:12,077
dollars,

131
00:06:12,087 --> 00:06:14,507
after an auction between tech
giants.

132
00:06:15,333 --> 00:06:16,133
Almost overnight,

133
00:06:16,753 --> 00:06:18,793
the whole field switched to deep
learning.

134
00:06:19,433 --> 00:06:22,583
Within three years, networks
were beating the human benchmark

135
00:06:22,593 --> 00:06:24,193
on the ImageNet test.

136
00:06:25,083 --> 00:06:28,303
That human benchmark came from
one dedicated researcher,

137
00:06:28,883 --> 00:06:32,193
Andrej Karpathy, who sat down
and labelled

138
00:06:32,203 --> 00:06:33,783
the test images himself.

139
00:06:34,443 --> 00:06:36,503
He got about five percent of
them wrong.

140
00:06:37,333 --> 00:06:38,833
Inside a trained network,

141
00:06:39,593 --> 00:06:42,763
the first layers detect simple
edges and colours,

142
00:06:42,773 --> 00:06:45,423
just like Hubel and Wiesel's
cells.

143
00:06:45,433 --> 00:06:48,653
Deeper layers respond to eyes,
wheels,

144
00:06:49,133 --> 00:06:49,723
fur,

145
00:06:49,733 --> 00:06:50,533
and faces.

146
00:06:52,067 --> 00:06:54,367
Today, computer vision is
everywhere.

147
00:06:55,167 --> 00:06:58,457
It unlocks your phone with your
face, and sorts the photos in

148
00:06:58,467 --> 00:06:59,267
your gallery.

149
00:06:59,750 --> 00:07:02,900
It helps doctors spot cancers
and eye disease

150
00:07:02,910 --> 00:07:03,750
in scans,

151
00:07:04,350 --> 00:07:07,110
sometimes catching what a busy
human might miss.

152
00:07:07,917 --> 00:07:09,597
It guides robots in warehouses,

153
00:07:10,237 --> 00:07:11,577
checks crops from drones,

154
00:07:12,177 --> 00:07:13,977
and lets cars detect
pedestrians,

155
00:07:14,617 --> 00:07:15,417
cyclists,

156
00:07:15,557 --> 00:07:16,377
and traffic lights.

157
00:07:17,250 --> 00:07:20,540
In some American cities, taxis
with no human

158
00:07:20,550 --> 00:07:22,670
driver now carry paying
passengers,

159
00:07:23,330 --> 00:07:26,820
using cameras, radar and laser
scanners to build a

160
00:07:26,830 --> 00:07:27,810
picture of the road.

161
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:31,790
Eye-screening systems can now
detect diabetic eye

162
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:34,720
disease from a photo of the back
of the eye,

163
00:07:35,220 --> 00:07:37,520
helping clinics that have too
few specialists.

164
00:07:38,667 --> 00:07:41,067
But machine vision has strange
weaknesses.

165
00:07:41,907 --> 00:07:45,277
Change a handful of pixels in a
way no human would

166
00:07:45,287 --> 00:07:46,077
notice,

167
00:07:46,087 --> 00:07:49,337
and a network can be fooled into
calling a panda a

168
00:07:49,347 --> 00:07:50,147
gibbon.

169
00:07:50,333 --> 00:07:52,153
And if the training pictures are
unbalanced,

170
00:07:52,733 --> 00:07:55,873
it can work worse for some
groups of people than others.

171
00:07:56,313 --> 00:07:59,193
Fixing that is one of the
biggest challenges in the field.

172
00:08:00,083 --> 00:08:03,493
In twenty fifteen, a photo app
wrongly tagged a

173
00:08:03,503 --> 00:08:06,883
photo of two Black people with
an offensive animal label.

174
00:08:07,243 --> 00:08:10,633
The company apologised, and its
quick fix was simply

175
00:08:10,643 --> 00:08:11,903
to block that label.

176
00:08:13,483 --> 00:08:16,513
In twenty eighteen, Hinton,
LeCun and

177
00:08:16,523 --> 00:08:18,933
Yoshua Bengio won the Turing
Award,

178
00:08:18,943 --> 00:08:22,353
computing's highest prize, for
their work on deep

179
00:08:22,363 --> 00:08:23,163
learning.

180
00:08:23,417 --> 00:08:26,787
And in twenty twenty four,
Hinton shared the Nobel Prize

181
00:08:26,797 --> 00:08:30,247
in Physics with John Hopfield,
for discoveries

182
00:08:30,257 --> 00:08:33,517
that made machine learning with
neural networks possible.

183
00:08:34,417 --> 00:08:37,627
The same ideas that taught
machines to see now power

184
00:08:37,637 --> 00:08:40,527
systems that understand speech,
translate

185
00:08:40,537 --> 00:08:41,337
languages,

186
00:08:41,777 --> 00:08:42,577
and write text.

187
00:08:44,233 --> 00:08:47,503
It took more than half a
century, a trick borrowed from a

188
00:08:47,513 --> 00:08:50,683
cat's brain, a contest built on
fourteen

189
00:08:50,693 --> 00:08:53,923
million photos, and two graphics
cards in a

190
00:08:53,933 --> 00:08:54,733
student's bedroom.

191
00:08:55,583 --> 00:08:58,633
But machines finally learned to
do what a three year old does

192
00:08:58,643 --> 00:08:59,443
without thinking:

193
00:09:00,183 --> 00:09:02,663
look at the world, and
understand what they see.
